%% Sequence Diagram — Refund a Failed Payment
%% Two entry points share one refund path: a biller failure callback (automatic release),
%% and a support employee refunding an Unknown or settled payment (manual, audited).

sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant B as Biller System
    actor S as Support Employee
    participant API as PayLink API
    participant PP as Payment Processing
    participant W as Wallet
    participant L as Ledger
    participant DB as Database
    participant N as Notifications

    alt automatic release after a biller failure
        B->>API: callback with paymentId, status=Failed, reason
        API->>PP: HandleBillerCallback(callbackId, paymentId)

        PP->>DB: has callbackId been processed
        alt duplicate callback
            DB-->>PP: already processed
            PP-->>API: 200 ignored, idempotent
        else first time
            PP->>DB: load payment and lock row
            PP->>PP: check status allows refund
            PP->>W: Release(amount + fee)
            W->>L: append Release entries
            PP->>DB: payment = Refunded with reason
            PP->>N: Notify customer refunded
            PP-->>API: 200 done
        end
    else manual refund from support
        S->>API: POST /payments/{id}/refund with reason
        API->>PP: ManualRefund(paymentId, reason, actor)
        PP->>DB: load payment and verify status is Unknown or Completed
        alt not refundable
            PP-->>API: 409 conflict
            API-->>S: cannot refund in this state
        else refundable
            PP->>W: Release held funds or reverse capture
            W->>L: append reversal entries
            PP->>DB: payment = Refunded with audit actor and reason
            PP->>N: Notify customer refunded
            PP-->>API: 200 done
            API-->>S: refund confirmed
        end
    end

    Note over PP,DB: every refund writes a ledger entry and an audit record,<br/>and a payment is refunded at most once.
